---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import AreaLookup from "../components/AreaLookup.astro";
---
<Base
  title="area &amp; route flood-evidence lookup · FloodWatch.PH"
  pathname="/lookup"
  description="Offline, in-browser lookup of dated flood evidence for a place or a route across Greater Metro Manila and the SLEX/NLEX corridor. Observed and modeled layers, each as-of-dated. Not a forecast, not a safety instruction. Your query never leaves your browser."
>
  <section class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-6 pt-8 pb-2">
    <p class="text-xs uppercase tracking-[0.2em] text-ink-500 mb-2 font-mono">
      the recurrence-vs-record gap, as a tool
    </p>
    <h1 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl mb-3 leading-tight text-ink-900">
      Is this place modeled-prone but thinly recorded?
    </h1>
    <p class="text-ink-700 leading-relaxed">
      Type a place, barangay, landmark, or expressway interchange in Greater
      Metro Manila, or pick two points to test a corridor. FloodWatch states
      the recurrence-vs-record gap as one conservative sentence, then shows the
      dated evidence behind it: the modeled recurrence score, the historical
      observed record, the most recent observed satellite pass, and rainfall
      and expressway context. It is not a flood score and not a verdict. Each
      row is stamped with its own date. National coverage beyond the
      SLEX/NLEX corridor is a later refinement.
    </p>
    <p class="text-[13px] text-ink-500 mt-3 leading-relaxed">
      FloodWatch is independent, open, and reproducible, and is complementary to
      and never a replacement for PAGASA, Project NOAH, MMDA Flood Control, and
      Google Flood Hub. It never forecasts. The whole lookup runs in your
      browser against a bundled offline place index; your query is never
      transmitted, logged, or republished.
    </p>
  </section>

  <AreaLookup />

  <section class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-6 py-6 text-xs text-ink-500 leading-relaxed">
    <p class="mb-2">
      Evidence assembled in-browser from the same public layers the rest of the
      site publishes: Track B recurrence (AlphaEarth 2017 embedding, 300 m
      grid), the Global Flood Database historical record (2002-2017), the most
      recent Sentinel-1 SAR pass (Copernicus, ~6-day revisit, ~24 h product latency, observed not
      live), GPM IMERG rainfall accumulation context, and the OpenStreetMap
      expressway network intersected with the latest observed extent. Place
      coordinates are public OpenStreetMap / PSA reference points.
    </p>
    <p class="mb-2 italic">
      Observed flood extent derived from public satellite data. Patterns may
      have legitimate explanations; figures warrant independent verification.
      Not an official hazard map. Not a forecast. Not routing or safety advice.
    </p>
    <p>
      Cite as: FloodWatch.PH (2026),
      <a href="https://github.com/xmpuspus/floodwatch-ph" class="text-accent-steel no-underline">github.com/xmpuspus/floodwatch-ph</a>.
      CC-BY-4.0 on data.
    </p>
  </section>
</Base>
